@page "/markdown"
@inject ISednaUi Ui

<CatHead Title="Markdown" Tier="@CatHead.Tier1">
    Two independent pieces: <code>.markdown-body</code> styles rendered Markdown wherever it
    appears, and <code>.md-editor</code> is a toolbar + textarea + live preview driven by
    <code>sednaUi.md</code>.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Markdown.MarkdownBody" Title="`.markdown-body`" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Put it on the container holding rendered HTML. It normalises headings, lists, code,
        quotes and links onto the tokens, and strips the first and last margin so it sits flush
        in a card body.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Markdown.MarkdownTable" Title="Tables and nested lists" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A table in rendered Markdown gets <a href="/table"><code>.table</code></a>'s own borders and
        header treatment without the class, and nested lists indent one step per level. The HTML here
        comes from somewhere else, so a long <code>&lt;code&gt;</code> breaks inside a word and a
        wide table scrolls rather than widening the page.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Markdown.MdEditor" Title="`.md-editor`" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live below</strong> — type in it, use the toolbar, then switch to Preview. The
        wiring is <code>data-md-view</code> on the root, <code>data-md-input</code> on the textarea,
        <code>data-md-preview</code> on the preview pane, <code>data-md-cmd</code> on toolbar
        buttons and <code>data-md-tab</code> on each radio of the
        <a href="/tabs"><code>.segmented</code></a> Write/Preview switch;
        <strong>the markup sits inert until <code>sednaUi.md.init()</code> runs</strong> — from C#,
        <code>ISednaUi.InitMarkdownAsync()</code> in <code>OnAfterRenderAsync</code>, which wires
        every editor in the document and is idempotent per editor. In Blazor, bind the textarea with
        <code>@@bind:event="oninput"</code> and let it own the value; toolbar edits dispatch a
        bubbling <code>input</code> event so the binding picks them up.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Markdown.EditorField" Title="As a form field" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live below.</strong> The editor in place: a <code>.form-label</code> pointing at the
        textarea's own id, a <code>.form-hint</code> reached by <code>aria-describedby</code>, and the
        <a href="/form"><code>.form-actions</code></a> row. Label the textarea, not the
        <code>.md-editor</code> wrapper — the textarea is the control — and leave out the
        <code>data-md-cmd</code> buttons the field does not need.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<div class="callout callout-danger">
    <i class="ri-shield-check-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Sanitise server-side as well</span>
        <code>sednaUi.md.render()</code> escapes all HTML <em>first</em> and only re-introduces a fixed
        set of constructs, with link hrefs restricted to <code>http:</code>, <code>https:</code>,
        <code>mailto:</code> and root-relative paths. It is not a spec-complete parser: for untrusted
        input rendered to other users, sanitise server-side as well.
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    // The one thing on this page that genuinely needs the circuit. An interop call
    // cannot run during prerendering, so it goes here rather than in OnInitializedAsync
    // — and the editor renders identically before it, which is the invariant this site
    // holds to: interactivity adds behaviour, it is never required to see an example.
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender) await Ui.InitMarkdownAsync();
    }
}
